<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Security Assistance Force</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2001–2014</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as ISAF</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Security Assistance Force was a multinational military mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> in December 2001, first to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> took command in 2003 and extended it across the country, where it fought the Taliban insurgency alongside Afghan forces, with some 130,000 troops at its peak. It ended in 2014 and was followed by a smaller training mission.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-security-assistance-force.jpg" alt="International Security Assistance Force" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: North Atlantic Treaty Organization · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_International_Security_Assistance_Force.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
